# Deployment configuration. Copy to .env and fill in; .env is gitignored.
#
#   npx hardhat run scripts/deploy.js --network bohr        # testnet, chain 968
#   npx hardhat run scripts/deploy.js --network botchain    # mainnet, chain 677
#
# Nothing here is needed for `hardhat test` or `hardhat compile`.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- signing
# The deployer. Funded from https://faucet.botchain.ai on Bohr.
# This account owns the protocol unless OWNER is set below, so on mainnet it should be a key you
# are willing to have hold ownership for as long as it takes to hand over to a multisig.
PRIVATE_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- roles
# Protocol owner. Defaults to the deployer.
# Set this to a multisig and every owner-only wiring call is deferred rather than executed — the
# deploy still writes a manifest, with the outstanding calls listed in `pendingOwnerCalls`, and
# the protocol does not work until they are executed from that address. That is the intended
# path for mainnet; on testnet leaving it unset is usually what you want.
# OWNER=

# Receives protocol fees and slash residue. Defaults to the deployer.
# TREASURY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- bond token
# REQUIRED on any non-local network — the deploy refuses to invent one.
#
# USDT, 6 decimals. Uncomment the ONE line matching the network you are deploying to. There is
# deliberately no shared default, because the two addresses differ and crossing them is not a
# mistake that announces itself:
#
#   Bohr testnet   968   0x75edC9335175Fc0552D51D48439F229c10420fe3   USDT, 6dp
#   BOT Chain      677   0xaBabc7Ddc03e501d190C676BF3d92ef0e6e87a3C   USDT, 6dp
#
# Use the mainnet address on Bohr and you do not get a revert — that address holds WES, a live
# unrelated 18-decimal token. decimals() returns 18, every capital parameter below is scaled a
# trillion times too high, and the deploy reports success. Both addresses were verified by
# calling symbol()/decimals() against each chain's own RPC.
#
# BOND_TOKEN=0x75edC9335175Fc0552D51D48439F229c10420fe3   # Bohr testnet
# BOND_TOKEN=0xaBabc7Ddc03e501d190C676BF3d92ef0e6e87a3C   # BOT Chain mainnet

# Only needed for a token that does not implement decimals(). Every capital parameter below is
# scaled by this, and getting it wrong does not revert — it silently freezes scores and prices
# challenges out of reach. The deploy reads it from the token and refuses to guess. USDT
# implements decimals(), so leave this alone.
# BOND_TOKEN_DECIMALS=6

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- capital parameters
# Whole tokens. Scaled by the bond token's decimals at deploy time; the defaults are shown.
#
# The bond token is USDT, so these read as dollars. MIN_BOND and CHALLENGE_BOND were repriced on
# 2026-08-11 for that: $100 to register an agent, $50 to dispute a delivery. A failed challenge is
# forfeited to the agent owner, so the pair is what sets the cost of griefing — the cheapest
# possible campaign burns $50 a time against an agent that staked twice that to exist.
#
# If you raise CHALLENGE_BOND, raise it knowing which way it fails. Too low is noisy and the noise
# is visible. Too high is quiet: nobody can afford to dispute, Bronze and Silver stop being
# challengeable, and challengeability is the only thing making them honest. Nothing reverts.
#
# HALF_WEIGHT and WEIGHT_CAP set how fast scores move rather than what anything costs, and they
# are sized against the SMALLEST agent the protocol admits, not the largest. A new Bronze agent
# bonding the $100 minimum sits at NEUTRAL, which unlocks 1.0x, and Bronze halves that — so its
# whole credit line is $50 and nothing it does can be bigger. At the old HALF_WEIGHT=100000 each
# of its deliveries counted for 0.05% and it needed ~1,400 of them to move its score halfway: it
# could not earn the score that unlocks the capital that would let it earn the score. At 1000 the
# same $50 delivery carries 4.8% and eleven clean ones reach the 2.0x band, which doubles its
# credit — the ramp accelerates itself from there.
#
# WEIGHT_CAP is ten times HALF_WEIGHT, so the biggest execution that counts fully is $10,000 and
# one delivery can move a score at most 91% of the way. The ratio is unchanged from the original
# design; whether 91% is too much history for a single execution to overwrite is a real question,
# but it is a different one from what scale these sit at.
# HALF_WEIGHT=1000             # notional at which one execution moves the score halfway
# WEIGHT_CAP=10000             # cap on a single execution's weight
# MIN_BOND=100                 # minimum bond to register an agent
# GLOBAL_NOTIONAL_CAP=5000000  # protocol-wide open exposure ceiling
# CHALLENGE_BOND=50            # bond required to challenge a delivery

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- input feeds
# Publishers whose quorum signs feed bundles. Comma-separated.
# Without at least one, no input bundle can be attested and no execution can be requested, so
# this is effectively required before the protocol is usable — it is left empty because the
# addresses are an operational decision, not a default.
# PUBLISHERS=
# PUBLISHER_QUORUM=1
# INPUT_MAX_AGE=300            # seconds a feed reading stays fresh

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- tiers
# Addresses allowed to enroll Silver-tier enclave measurements.
# TEE_NOTARIES=

# Gold tier. "false" skips the ZK adapter, its verifier and the model binding entirely.
# Bronze and Silver are unaffected, but challenge escalation then has no court of last resort.
# DEPLOY_GOLD=false

# Reuse an already-deployed ezkl verifier instead of deploying circuits/build/Verifier.sol.
# EZKL_VERIFIER=

# Bind the verifier to a different model than circuits/spec.json describes. The commitment is
# keccak256 of the model name and must match what the agent registered with; the scale bits are
# the circuit's `input_scale`. Both are read from spec.json by default, and guessing either makes
# every honest Gold proof for that model fail closed.
# MODEL_COMMITMENT=
# INPUT_SCALE_BITS=
